# Keep the happy zone boring to test

The happy zone is application decisions: explicit input, explicit output, no hidden globals. Unit tests of that code do not boot jsdom, do not use fake timers as a runner trick, and do not touch the network.

Time is an injected dependency. Pass a clock or a `Date` into the decision. Do not read `new Date()` or `Date.now()` inside the happy zone.
See time-is-an-external-dependency.mdc and inject-wall-clock.mdc.

Adapters, UI, and broker clients live outside. Test them at their own boundary with controlled inputs. Do not pull React, `fetch`, or Alpaca into a unit test of a rule.

```typescript
import assert from 'node:assert';
import { ok } from 'true-myth/result';

type Grid = { expiresAt: Date };

// ❌ Rule reads the clock and the network
async function shouldFlip(grid: Grid): Promise<boolean> {
    const quote = await fetch('https://api.alpaca.markets/v2/stocks/AAPL/quotes/latest');
    return Date.now() > grid.expiresAt.getTime() && quote.ok;
}

// ✅ Pure rule. Mocha needs no jsdom, fake timers, or HTTP.
function shouldFlip(grid: Grid, now: Date) {
    return ok(now.getTime() > grid.expiresAt.getTime());
}

suite('shouldFlip', function () {
    test('is false before expiry', function () {
        const result = shouldFlip(
            { expiresAt: new Date('2026-09-02T00:00:00Z') },
            new Date('2026-09-01T00:00:00Z')
        );
        assert.strictEqual(result.unwrapOr(true), false);
    });
});
```
